            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>200B parameters in bf16 across 32 TPU v4p. What is the lower bound on per-step sampling latency?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[2\ \text {bytes} \times  200\mathrm {e}9 = 400\mathrm {e}9 \text { bytes} \;\longrightarrow \; \frac {12.5\mathrm {e}9 \text { bytes/chip}}{1.23\mathrm {e}12 \text { B/s}} \approx  10\ \mathrm {ms}]]></fr:tex>

  It is a bound because every decode step has to re-read <html:em>all</html:em> the parameters from <fr:link href="/AI2G/" title="High bandwidth memory" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AI2G/" display-uri="AI2G" type="local">HBM</fr:link> into the MXU, so no step can beat the weight-load time. At small batch it is close to achievable, too: decode is HBM-bandwidth-bound, not FLOPs-bound, which is the whole reason <fr:link href="/UZ6D/" title="The batch size is the intensity" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/UZ6D/" display-uri="UZ6D" type="local">batching pays</fr:link>.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
